DRAM on package. The wires are short and the power is much lower. Upto 8.5 GT/s. 
Two tiles/chiplets - CPU tile and PCT tile.
Tile is Intel’s term for what the industry generally calls a chiplet: a separate piece of silicon (die) that does one job, and several of which are assembled into one package. Instead of one big monolithic chip, the processor is built from smaller dies joined together with advanced packaging.
CPU tile : Compute tile
PCT tile: Platform controller tile - deals with IO and platform functions
Why split it up: you can use the expensive leading-edge process only for the part that benefits, mix and match dies from different fabs, and improve yield, since small dies have fewer defects than one huge die.
